# People, ECO 2026 tasks 4 to 8: engage stakeholders, align and manage stakeholder expectations,
# help ensure knowledge transfer, plan and manage communication.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "The project charter has just been approved and you have been assigned as project manager. Which should you do next?",
      ["Identify the stakeholders and record them in a stakeholder register",
       "Baseline the schedule and send it to the functional managers for resources",
       "Hold a lessons learned session with the managers of earlier projects",
       "Ask the sponsor to approve the detailed cost estimates for all work packages"],
      "Identifying stakeholders starts as soon as the charter is approved, because their needs shape every plan that follows. A baseline and detailed cost approval come after planning, and reviewing past lessons helps but does not replace knowing who the stakeholders are."),
    X(1, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "Using a power/interest grid, select the engagement approach for each stakeholder.",
      ["The sponsor, who controls the budget and reviews progress every week",
       "A regional director who can stop the project but rarely shows interest in it",
       "Warehouse staff who will use the system daily but cannot change project decisions",
       "A department that is barely affected and has little influence on the project"],
      ["Manage closely", "Keep satisfied", "Keep informed", "Monitor"],
      [0, 1, 2, 3],
      "High power and high interest: manage closely. High power, low interest: keep satisfied. Low power, high interest: keep informed. Low power, low interest: monitor with minimal effort."),
    S(1, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "A",
      "A department head is openly resisting your agile project and has told her staff not to attend the story-writing workshops. What should you do first?",
      ["Meet with the department head to understand the concerns behind her resistance",
       "Ask the sponsor to instruct the department head to send her staff to the workshops",
       "Hold the workshops without her department and record the decision in the issue log",
       "Escalate the resistance to the steering committee as a risk that the project cannot control"],
      "Engaging a resistant stakeholder starts with understanding her concerns; there may be a legitimate issue the project should address. Using the sponsor's authority or escalating comes later, and leaving her department out risks requirements that her staff will later reject."),
    S(1, 1, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "You want a simple tool that compares each stakeholder's current level of engagement with the level the project needs. Which tool should you use?",
      ["Stakeholder engagement assessment matrix",
       "Responsibility assignment matrix for the project team",
       "Requirements traceability matrix",
       "Probability and impact matrix"],
      "The stakeholder engagement assessment matrix records current (C) and desired (D) engagement, such as unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive or leading, which shows where engagement work is needed. The other matrices cover roles, requirements and risks."),
    N(1, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "P",
      "Your project team is growing from 8 people to 12 people. How many additional communication channels will this create? Type the number only.",
      ["38"],
      "Channels = n(n - 1) / 2. Eight people have 8 x 7 / 2 = 28 channels; twelve have 12 x 11 / 2 = 66. The increase is 66 - 28 = 38, which is why adding people adds communication effort quickly."),
    S(1, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "Executives say your status reports are too long, while the delivery teams say the same reports leave out details they need. What should you do?",
      ["Tailor the reporting: a one-page summary for executives and detailed reports for the teams",
       "Keep a single report for everyone, so that no stakeholder group ever misses any information",
       "Replace the written reports with one weekly meeting that every stakeholder group must attend",
       "Shorten the report for everyone and ask team members to find missing details on their own"],
      "Communication should be tailored to each audience's needs: decision-level summaries for executives, working detail for teams. A single report satisfies neither group, a mandatory all-hands meeting wastes time, and shortening for everyone leaves the teams short of information."),
    S(1, 1, "Plan and manage communication", "A",
      "Stakeholders keep interrupting your agile team to ask about progress. What is the best way to reduce these interruptions?",
      ["Keep a visible task board and burndown chart that anyone can check at any time",
       "Ask stakeholders to send all questions to the product owner in a weekly email",
       "Tell the team to decline every question from stakeholders until the sprint ends",
       "Send the stakeholders a detailed written report of every task at the end of each day"],
      "Information radiators make progress visible without interrupting the team. Weekly emails slow the flow of information, refusing all questions harms relationships, and daily detailed reports add overhead the team does not need."),
    S(1, 2, "Help ensure knowledge transfer", "H",
      "The only engineer who understands the legacy billing interface will leave the company in three weeks. The interface must be changed in the next release. What should you do?",
      ["Have the engineer pair with team members and document the critical knowledge before leaving",
       "Ask human resources to delay the engineer's departure date until the next release has gone live",
       "Plan to hire an external specialist after the engineer leaves to rebuild the missing knowledge",
       "Capture the engineer's knowledge in the lessons learned session planned for project closure"],
      "The knowledge is critical and leaving soon, so the project manager arranges transfer now through pairing and documentation. Delaying a resignation is usually not possible, rebuilding later costs time, and lessons learned at closure is far too late."),
    M(1, 2, "Help ensure knowledge transfer", "A",
      "Which TWO practices help knowledge flow across an agile team? (Choose two.)",
      ["Pairing team members on tasks",
       "Retrospectives with recorded actions",
       "One specialist owning each technical area",
       "Collecting lessons only at project closure",
       "Limiting wiki editing rights to team leads"],
      2,
      "Pairing spreads skills between people, and retrospectives turn experience into shared improvements during the project. Single owners per area create knowledge silos, lessons captured only at closure cannot help this project, and restricted editing discourages sharing."),
    S(1, 2, "Manage stakeholder expectations", "H",
      "After a product demonstration, the customer's satisfaction score dropped sharply, although the team delivered every item in the sprint goal. What should you do first?",
      ["Meet with the customer to learn which expectations the increment did not meet",
       "Point out to the customer that every item in the agreed sprint goal was delivered",
       "Ask the team to add more features to the next sprint so that the score recovers",
       "Report the low score to the sponsor and ask for help in managing the customer's views"],
      "A drop in satisfaction means an expectation gap; the first step is to understand it. Defending the sprint goal does not close the gap, adding features may miss the real concern, and escalating before understanding the cause is premature."),
    S(1, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "On your hybrid project, the sponsor tells you that your monthly reports are accurate but do not help him. What should you do?",
      ["Ask the sponsor which decisions he needs to make and adjust the report to support them",
       "Add more detail to the monthly reports so that the sponsor has every piece of information",
       "Ask the PMO for its standard report template and use it for the sponsor from next month",
       "Keep the reports as they are, because they are accurate and meet the governance standard"],
      "A feedback loop means using the receiver's response to improve communication; reports should be aligned with what the sponsor needs to decide. More detail or a generic template may still miss the need, and accuracy alone does not make a report useful."),
    S(1, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "H",
      "Midway through your hybrid project, a contact at a government regulator asks to be kept informed because the project affects a licence the company holds. What should you do?",
      ["Add the contact to the stakeholder register, analyze their needs and update the engagement plan",
       "Forward the request to the legal department, since dealing with regulators is outside the project",
       "Send the contact the latest status report and add them to the existing distribution list",
       "Tell the contact that the stakeholder list was finalized during planning and cannot be changed"],
      "Stakeholder identification continues throughout the project. A new stakeholder is recorded, analyzed and planned for, which may call for a different communication approach than the standard report. Ignoring the regulator or handing it off without analysis creates risk."),
    S(1, 3, "Align stakeholder expectations", "H",
      "The sales director expects the new portal to launch with full ordering features, while the support director expects a smaller launch with fewer support calls. Both are key stakeholders. What should you do?",
      ["Facilitate a discussion between them about expectations against the project objectives and agree priorities",
       "Follow the sales director's expectations, since sales brings in the revenue that pays for the project",
       "Plan the scope to satisfy both directors fully, adding whatever time and budget to the baseline is needed",
       "Ask the sponsor to decide between the two directors without first discussing it with either of them"],
      "Aligning expectations means facilitating a discussion that tests both against the project objectives and reaches agreed priorities. Picking a side or trying to satisfy everything avoids the conversation, and going to the sponsor before facilitating skips the project manager's role."),
    S(1, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "P",
      "A key stakeholder complains that she was never told about an approved change that affects her department. What should you do first?",
      ["Review the communication management plan to see how her information needs were captured",
       "Apologize and send her the change log so she can review all approved changes herself",
       "Ask the change control board to explain to her why the change was approved without her input",
       "Add her to every project distribution list so that she never misses information again"],
      "The first step is to find the cause: whether her needs were missed in the communication plan or the plan was not followed. Sending a log or adding her to every list treats the symptom, and the change board did not cause the communication gap."),
    S(1, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "H",
      "Read the case study. What is the best way to deal with the shift supervisors at the largest site?",
      ["Meet with the supervisors to hear their concerns and involve them in testing the new picking workflows",
       "Ask the VP of Operations to tell the supervisors that the decision is final and they must support it",
       "Send the supervisors the business case so that they can see the productivity gains the system will bring",
       "Delay go-live at the largest site until the supervisors stop telling their teams that picking will slow"],
      "The supervisors were missed during stakeholder analysis and now influence many users. Engaging them, hearing their concerns and involving them in testing builds ownership and may reveal real workflow problems. Using authority, sending documents or simply waiting does not address their concerns.",
      stim="PMP-CS1", step=1),

    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 1, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "A group of field technicians has just been identified as stakeholders. What should you do before choosing how to communicate with them?",
      ["Analyze their interests, influence and information needs",
       "Add them to the existing weekly status email distribution",
       "Ask the sponsor which communication method to use with them",
       "Wait until they ask for information so that effort is not wasted"],
      "Stakeholder analysis comes before planning engagement and communication, so the method fits their needs. Defaulting to the existing email, deferring to the sponsor or waiting for requests all skip that analysis."),
    S(2, 2, "Align stakeholder expectations", "A",
      "A client manager who is new to agile attends sprint reviews but says very little and later sends long lists of complaints by email. What should you do?",
      ["Coach the client manager on the purpose of sprint reviews and the kind of feedback that helps most",
       "Ask the product owner to stop inviting the client manager to sprint reviews and to send summaries instead",
       "Ask the team to answer each emailed complaint in writing before starting work on the next sprint",
       "Escalate the emails to the client's sponsor as evidence that the client manager is not engaged"],
      "Mentoring and coaching stakeholders on how to take part is part of aligning expectations; once the client manager knows how reviews work, feedback can be given and discussed in the review. Excluding the person, answering by email or escalating does not build engagement."),
    S(2, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "At the last steering meeting, a key stakeholder was surprised by a schedule delay that you announced, and she has been cool toward the project since. What should you do differently?",
      ["Brief her early and privately about significant bad news before it reaches formal meetings",
       "Leave bad news out of the steering meetings and report it in the written monthly report only",
       "Ask the sponsor to deliver all bad news to her in future, since the sponsor has more authority",
       "Present only recovery plans in future meetings and leave the delays themselves out of them"],
      "Trust is built by transparency and no surprises; key stakeholders should hear significant news early and in the right setting. Hiding or softening bad news damages trust further, and handing the message to the sponsor avoids the project manager's responsibility."),
    S(2, 2, "Manage stakeholder expectations", "A",
      "How should an agile team best keep track of whether its customer remains satisfied as the product evolves?",
      ["Gather feedback at each sprint review and track satisfaction over time",
       "Run one detailed satisfaction survey when the final release is done",
       "Assume satisfaction for as long as the customer raises no formal complaints",
       "Ask the team members to rate how happy they believe the customer is"],
      "Frequent feedback at reviews, tracked over time, lets the team respond while it can still change course. A single final survey is too late, silence does not mean satisfaction, and the team's guess is not the customer's view."),
    X(2, 1, "Plan and manage communication", "P",
      "Select the communication method each example uses.",
      ["A weekly status email sent to a distribution list",
       "A project wiki that stakeholders read when they choose",
       "A video call to resolve a disputed requirement",
       "Recorded training videos on the intranet",
       "A workshop to agree on acceptance criteria"],
      ["Interactive", "Push", "Pull"],
      [1, 2, 0, 2, 0],
      "Push communication is sent to recipients (emails, memos). Pull communication is placed where people access it when they choose (wikis, intranet libraries). Interactive communication is a real-time exchange (calls, meetings, workshops) and is best for resolving disagreements and reaching agreement."),
    S(2, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "Your stakeholders are spread across five time zones, and many complain that the weekly live status meeting is at an unreasonable hour for them. What should you do?",
      ["Publish a short recorded update with written highlights, and keep live sessions only for decisions",
       "Keep the meeting time as it is, since any time chosen will be inconvenient for somebody on the team",
       "Move the meeting each week to a new time slot and require every stakeholder to attend each session",
       "Cancel the status meeting and ask stakeholders to contact the team members directly when needed"],
      "Asynchronous updates suit routine status across time zones, and live time is saved for decisions that need interaction. Ignoring the complaint, adding mandatory rotating meetings or sending people to the team directly all make communication worse."),
    S(2, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "Your team has found a defect that will push the release date back by two weeks. The sponsor is preparing to announce the release date to customers tomorrow. What should you do?",
      ["Tell the sponsor today about the defect, its impact and the recovery options",
       "Let the announcement go ahead and look for ways to recover the two weeks later",
       "Ask the team to work through the weekend before deciding whether to tell anyone",
       "Mention the defect in the next monthly report as a schedule risk to be monitored"],
      "Transparency requires sharing bad news promptly, especially before the sponsor commits the organization publicly. Letting the announcement go ahead, hoping overtime fixes it, or downgrading a known delay to a risk in a later report all breach that duty."),
    M(2, 1, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "Which TWO are main purposes of a project kickoff meeting? (Choose two.)",
      ["Align stakeholders on the objectives, scope and success criteria",
       "Introduce the roles, responsibilities and ways of communicating",
       "Baseline the detailed schedule for every work package",
       "Approve the change requests submitted so far in the project",
       "Close the procurement agreements with the chosen suppliers"],
      2,
      "A kickoff builds a shared understanding of objectives and success and sets out who does what and how the project will communicate. Baselining, approving changes and closing procurements are separate project activities with their own processes."),
    S(2, 3, "Manage stakeholder expectations", "A",
      "Without talking to the team, the product owner promised a key customer that a large new feature would be ready in the next release. The team believes it cannot be done in time. What should you do?",
      ["Help the product owner and team size the feature, then present the customer with realistic options",
       "Ask the team to commit to the feature anyway so that the product owner's promise to the customer is kept",
       "Tell the customer directly that the product owner made a mistake and that the feature will not be ready",
       "Remove the feature from the backlog until the customer agrees to wait for a release after the next one"],
      "The expectation was set without the people who do the work; the right move is to estimate the work with the team and then reset the customer's expectations with real options, supporting the product owner rather than undermining them. Forcing a commitment risks quality and burnout."),
    S(2, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "Your PMO requires a monthly earned value report, but the agile team tracks progress with release burnup charts. What is the best approach?",
      ["Agree with the PMO how the agile measures map to its reporting needs and report on that basis",
       "Ask the team to stop using burnup charts and track their work in earned value terms instead",
       "Ignore the PMO requirement, because earned value reporting does not apply to agile teams",
       "Produce the earned value report yourself each month without telling the team how the figures are built"],
      "Hybrid reporting works by agreeing with governance bodies how adaptive measures (for example completed story points against the release plan) satisfy their needs. Forcing one method on the other, ignoring governance or reporting behind the team's back all cause problems."),
    S(2, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "A",
      "A key business user has stopped attending sprint reviews, and recent increments have drifted from what her department needs. What should you do first?",
      ["Find out why she has stopped attending and adjust the review's timing or format if needed",
       "Ask her manager to make attendance at every sprint review a part of her job objectives",
       "Ask the team to build to the original requirements until she starts attending reviews again",
       "Send her a recording of every review and ask her to send written feedback within two days"],
      "Understanding why a stakeholder has disengaged comes first; the fix may be as simple as changing the time or making the review more relevant. Pressure through her manager, ignoring her needs or replacing discussion with recordings does not address the cause."),
    S(2, 1, "Help ensure knowledge transfer", "P",
      "When should the lessons learned register be updated?",
      ["Throughout the project, whenever a lesson is identified",
       "Only at the end of each phase, during phase closure",
       "Only during project closure, once all of the work is accepted",
       "Only after an audit has identified a process problem"],
      "Lessons are captured as they arise so they can improve the current project as well as future ones. Waiting for phase or project closure loses detail and benefit, and audits are not the only source of lessons."),
    S(2, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "H",
      "Your hybrid project needs timely reviews from the IT security team before each release. That team does not report to you and has its own priorities. What is the best way to get its support?",
      ["Show how the project supports the security team's goals and agree a review process with them",
       "Ask the sponsor to order the security team to make the project's reviews its top priority",
       "Schedule the reviews in the project plan and then tell the security team when each one is due",
       "Skip the reviews when the team is late and note them as an accepted risk in the register"],
      "Influencing without authority works by building shared interest and agreeing a workable process. Using the sponsor's authority damages the relationship, scheduling others' work without agreement rarely works, and skipping required reviews creates compliance and security risk."),
    S(2, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "Team members have heard rumors that the project will be cancelled, and morale is falling. What should you do?",
      ["Confirm the facts with the sponsor, then share openly with the team what you can",
       "Tell the team that the rumors are false, even though you are unsure whether that is true",
       "Avoid the topic so that the rumors die down and the team stays focused on its work",
       "Ask the team members to report whoever started the rumors so that it does not happen again"],
      "Transparency means finding out the facts and sharing them honestly. Denying something you cannot confirm risks trust, silence lets rumors grow, and hunting for the source creates fear."),
    S(2, 2, "Plan and manage communication", "H",
      "Read the case study. Steering committee members say the monthly report is too long, and the city council will soon need its first quarterly update. How should you plan reporting for these two groups?",
      ["Decision-focused monthly reports for the committee and a plain quarterly summary of progress and benefits for the council",
       "One detailed report that goes to both groups, so that the council and the committee always see the same information",
       "Monthly reports only for the steering committee, with the general manager briefing the council from them as needed",
       "A full technical report for the council each quarter, since councillors may face detailed questions from residents about it"],
      "Reporting should be aligned with each audience: the committee governs the project and needs decision-focused reports, while the council needs a concise view of progress and the public benefits, such as water loss. One report for all misses both needs, and leaving the council to secondhand briefings ignores a key stakeholder.",
      stim="PMP-CS2", step=2),

    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Engage stakeholders", "P",
      "Which information belongs in the stakeholder register?",
      ["Each stakeholder's interests, influence and expectations",
       "Each team member's hourly rate and weekly timesheet totals",
       "The approved budget and its spending by month",
       "The defects found in testing and who fixed them"],
      "The stakeholder register records who the stakeholders are, their roles, interests, influence and expectations, and their classification. Rates, budgets and defects belong in other project documents."),
    S(0, 1, "Manage stakeholder expectations", "A",
      "During a sprint review, a customer asks for several changes to the increment. What should happen to these requests?",
      ["The product owner adds them to the product backlog and prioritizes them",
       "The team adds them to the current sprint so the customer sees them at once",
       "The project manager rejects them because the sprint scope is already fixed",
       "The customer submits a formal change request for the change control board"],
      "In agile, new requests become backlog items that the product owner orders by value. Adding them mid-sprint breaks the sprint goal, rejecting feedback defeats the purpose of reviews, and a formal change board is not how an agile backlog changes."),
    S(0, 1, "Plan and manage communication", "P",
      "In the sender-receiver model of communication, what confirms that a message was understood as intended?",
      ["Feedback from the receiver",
       "The medium used to send it",
       "Encoding by the sender",
       "Noise in the channel"],
      "Feedback (for example the receiver restating the message or asking questions) shows whether it was understood. The medium carries the message, encoding turns thoughts into a message, and noise interferes with it."),
    M(0, 2, "Engage stakeholders", "H",
      "On a hybrid project, which TWO actions best keep business stakeholders engaged? (Choose two.)",
      ["Invite them to sprint reviews to see working increments",
       "Share a milestone roadmap that shows the coming releases",
       "Send them the full backlog each week without a summary",
       "Limit contact with them to the end of each project phase",
       "Ask team members to email stakeholders on their own"],
      2,
      "Seeing working results and a clear roadmap keeps stakeholders involved and lets them steer. Raw backlog dumps overwhelm, phase-end-only contact misses chances to give feedback, and uncoordinated emails create confusion."),
]
